The [[Land Rover Series II|Series II]] Land Rover launched in 1958 was larger, heavier, and more complex than the original, and the need was ever-present for higher powered engines.<ref name="Taylor, 2007">Taylor, 2007</ref> Also, the Rover petrol engines in use at the time, with the archaic [[Rover IOE engine|inlet-over-exhaust]] valve layout, were approaching the age of 20 years in design terms. A new, larger petrol engine specifically developed for the Land Rover was needed.<ref>Taylor, 1988, p.35</ref> The existing 2-litre diesel engine was used as a basis, but with a radically changed internal structure.<ref name="Robson, 2003, p. 37">Robson, 2003, p. 37</ref> The new engine was a dry-liner type, and a wider bore was used to improve low-speed torque output.<ref>Dymock, 2006, p. 96</ref> Despite the numerous changes, the petrol engine could use the same machining line as the diesel, establishing a design commonly used between Land Rover's petrol and diesel engines that would survive for decades.<ref name="Taylor, 2007"/><ref name="Robson, 2003, p. 37"/> The 2.25-litre petrol was the most popular engine option right up to the mid-1980s, and established a worldwide reputation for reliability and longevity.<ref name="Taylor, 2007"/><ref>Hodder, 2000, p. 120</ref> The engine's relatively low compression ratio and general strong design made it tolerant of poor quality fuel and oil, as well as infrequent servicing. With proper maintenance, these engines can easily survive more than {{convert|250000|mi|km}} of service. This was partly due to the commonality between petrol and diesel versions making the petrol version somewhat over-engineered for the job; they retained the extraordinary strength characteristics of the diesel while being much less stressed. The engine castings were produced by Bean Industries Limited, a Sheffield-based foundry, using "[http://www.historywebsite.co.uk/articles/Tipton/Bean/Cars.htm Bilchrome]" an in-house developed iron for cylinders. The only major change to the design was the fitting of a five-bearing crankshaft in 1980, which improved bottom-end strength and refinement.<ref>Hodder, 2000, p. 119-applies to mileage life and crankshaft modification</ref> Despite its utilitarian origins, the 2.25-litre petrol is a quiet, smooth-running engine, and this enabled Rover to fit it to their P4 saloon car as the Rover 80.<ref>Dymock. 2006, p. 96</ref> Various power outputs were available for this engine depending on the compression ratio and the amount of emissions-regulation equipment fitted.<ref name="Taylor, 2007"/><ref>USA magazine ''Popular Imported Cars'' quoted {{cvt|65|hp}} for an emissions-controlled engine in a Jan. 1974 test.</ref><ref>Dymock, 2006 quotes as much as {{cvt|81|bhp}} in a few instances.</ref><ref>Robson, 2003,
p.38</ref>

'''Used in''': [[Land Rover Series II]], [[Land Rover Series III|Series III]], and [[Land Rover Defender 90|Ninety/One Ten/127]]; also [[Rover P4]] 80<ref>Dymock, 2006, p. 128</ref>

